The Loire by bike route runs right under the windows of Amboise, and it is one of the few French cycle routes where you can string together three châteaux without ever pushing hard. We watch these cyclists come through every season, and the same questions come back: how far, how hilly, where to sleep. Here are the routes we recommend from the Château de la Huberdière, with the real distances.

## Where does the Loire by bike route pass through Amboise?

The Loire by bike route crosses Amboise along the river, on the stage linking Blois to Tours, and the green and white waymarking needs no map. The main course runs along the bank, below the royal château, then heads west towards Montlouis-sur-Loire.

The full route belongs to EuroVelo 6, which links the Atlantic to the Black Sea. The Loire Valley has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2000, and that is exactly the landscape you ride through: the river, the vine-planted slopes, the villages of white tuffeau stone. The Val d'Amboise adds six waymarked loops of its own, around 150 km of circuits away from the main course.

We are on the right bank, at Nazelles-Négron, facing Amboise. The estate sits up on the slope, a few minutes from the route: the last stretch climbs, and that is the only hard part of the day.

![The château seen from the lake in the grounds, in spring, with a dandelion clock in the foreground](/images/inline/chateau-exterieur/chateau-exterieur-24.webp "The estate looks down on its lake from the slope at Nazelles-Négron. The climb up from the riverbank takes five minutes by bike, a little longer with panniers.")

## Amboise to Chenonceau: 20 km on loop 47

From Amboise to Chenonceau, count on about 20 km along waymarked loop n° 47, which leaves the Loire, crosses the forest of Amboise and drops into the Cher valley. It is the first ride we suggest to our guests.

The full loop, named la voie royale, runs to just under 47 km and returns to Amboise along the other bank. In the short version you ride to the château, visit, have lunch there and come back the same way. The forest breaks the wind and gives shade in summer, which the riverbank never does.

One piece of advice that applies to everyone: book your Chenonceau ticket online the day before. Arriving by bike in the coach queue, in the middle of August, ruins a fine morning.

## Amboise to Tours along the greenway

Heading for Tours, the Loire by bike route follows the river for around 30 km, on a greenway or quiet lanes for most of the way, with no climbing. It is the easiest stretch in the area, fine for children and occasional cyclists alike.

You ride through Montlouis-sur-Loire and its vineyards for a good 20 km. The cellars can be visited along the way, and several winemakers welcome cyclists without an appointment in season. Bring something to carry the bottles in, or have them delivered: we have watched enough cyclists wobble off to feel the need to say it.

Once in Tours, coming back by train is easy, since Tours and Saint-Pierre-des-Corps stations take bikes on most regional services.

## Amboise to Chaumont-sur-Loire: the finest of the three

Eastwards, Chaumont-sur-Loire lies about 20 km from Amboise going back up the river, and it is our favourite stretch around here. The road hugs the Loire, troglodyte houses open into the tuffeau, and the château appears all at once on its bluff.

One warning, though: this section is not a greenway all the way. Part of it runs on a shared country road, easy riding but with cars. With young children, take the Tours direction instead.

The International Garden Festival at Chaumont, open from April to November, justifies the day out on its own. Allow a good two hours there, and set off back before late afternoon if you are riding home in October, when night falls early.

![The valley poplars, bare at the end of the season, carrying clumps of mistletoe against an orange evening sky](/images/inline/chateau-exterieur/chateau-exterieur-09.webp "Late in the season, mistletoe shows up in the poplars as soon as the leaves drop. The low evening light arrives around 5 pm in October.")

## How long does the whole Loire by bike route take?

The full route measures 900 km, from Cuffy near Nevers to Saint-Brevin-les-Pins on the Atlantic, and seasoned cyclists ride it in twelve to fifteen days. That is the official figure from the Loire à Vélo route, run by the Centre-Val de Loire and Pays de la Loire regions.

Nobody has to ride all of it. The Touraine crossing takes two or three days, and that is what most of the people who stay with us choose. The Blois to Amboise section, a classic, runs 96 km and takes three days with the visits.

Our view, having seen it a hundred times: three days around Amboise beat eight days of chasing kilometres. On the Loire, it is the stops that make the trip.

## Which is the best part of the Loire by bike route?

The stretch between Amboise and Tours is, to our mind, the finest of the whole route, because it brings together the wild river, the vineyards and three châteaux in thirty kilometres. Few sections pack so much into a single day's riding.

Not everyone will agree. Lovers of open space prefer the Loire downstream of Tours, wider, emptier, where the river spreads across sandbanks. Upstream, towards Blois and Chambord, the landscape turns to forest and the châteaux grow more monumental. Here you get the Loire Valley in its lived-in version, with vines, villages and cellars.

## Where can you hire a bike in Amboise?

Several hire shops operate in Amboise and around the Val d'Amboise, with standard bikes, electric bikes and child trailers, by the half-day, the day or the week. Some will deliver to your accommodation.

Let us be straight about it: we do not hire out bikes at the château, and we are not going to pretend otherwise. What we do is book for you with the nearest shop before you arrive, and keep the bikes under cover during your stay. For a group, allow two weeks' notice in summer, as the electric ones go quickly.

## Which season should you choose?

From May to early October the Loire by bike route rides without nasty surprises, and the late season is what we prefer. In September and October the harvest is in full swing on the slopes of Vouvray and Montlouis, the light drops early over the river, and the paths are empty compared with July and August.

Summer remains high season, with a drawback few guides mention: the riverbanks have almost no shade. At 35 degrees, a 40 km stage becomes an ordeal. Set off early, or move over to the forest of Amboise and the Chenonceau loop.

In winter the route stays open and rideable, but the hire shops close and the days are short. It is a season for outings, not for stages.

## Where to sleep on the Loire by bike route near Amboise?

Sleeping a few minutes from the route, inside enclosed grounds, changes a day on the bike: you arrive, drop the panniers, and never get back in a car. The Château de la Huberdière is a 3-star charming hotel with 10 bedrooms, set in 14 hectares of woods, lakes and meadows at Nazelles-Négron, facing Amboise.

No two bedrooms are alike, and that is deliberate. The grounds are enclosed, with no neighbours and no passing traffic, and the heated pool does a great deal of good after 40 km. You will find the rooms and rates on the page to [book a stay at the château](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise), and an account of [a night in a Loire Valley château](/en/blog/sleep-in-a-loire-chateau) if you are still hesitating.

In the evening, the table is laid at the house. Sharing boards come out every night except Sunday, and on Thursdays dinner is entirely Italian, fresh pasta included, because that is where I come from. The page on [dining at the château](/en/chateau-dining-touraine) has the detail, and the piece on the [table d'hôtes in Touraine](/en/blog/table-dhotes-loire-chateau) tells you how it really works.

![A table laid outdoors along the château façade, white cloth, roses in round vases and a basket of bread](/images/inline/chateau-exterieur/chateau-exterieur-06.webp "Dinner is served outside whenever the weather allows, against the still-warm façade. Sharing boards come out every night except Sunday.")

## Three days of cycling around Amboise

Here is the plan we hand out most often, built on three nights and two full days in the saddle.

- Day 1: arrive, ride up to the château, loop around the grounds and their lakes to loosen the legs.
- Day 2: out to Chenonceau through the forest of Amboise, around 45 km with the visit, back in the late afternoon.
- Day 3: down to Montlouis-sur-Loire and its cellars, a 30 km round trip, with a tasting in the middle.
- Option: a day off the bike to [visit the Loire Valley châteaux](/en/blog/visiting-loire-chateaux) by car, since Chambord and Villandry are out of reach on a day's riding.

If you would rather take it gently, keep day 2 and swap day 3 for a morning in Amboise, at the royal château and the Clos Lucé, both ten minutes from here.

Tell us your dates and how many bikes, and we will come back with a proposal within 24 hours and the contact for the nearest hire shop.
